                <h1>Concussion Management</h1>
                <p class="subheader">Orthopaedics</p>
                <!--<p class="light"><strong>Related to:</strong> Hand plastic surgery, orthopaedics </p>-->
                <img class="img-margin margin-bottom" src="https://uthscsa.edu/physicians/sites/physicians/files/2025-11/Concussion%20Management.jpg" alt="">
                <h2>What is concussion management?</h2>
                <p>Concussions affect everyone differently. Treatments focus on managing symptoms to ensure a safe return to physical activity, work or school. At UT Health San Antonio, our board-certified sports medicine specialists customize a treatment plan tailored to your distinct needs. We offer the latest evidence-based assessments and treatments for patients of all ages, regardless of the cause of the concussion.</p>

                <h2>What is a concussion?</h2>
                <p>A <a href="https://uthscsa.staywellhealthlibrary.com/Search/134,14">concussion</a> is a mild traumatic brain injury that occurs from an impact to the head. A hard hit to the body that jostles the head can also cause a concussion. The force of this impact causes the brain to move around inside the skull. This movement can damage brain cells and affect how the brain works. </p>
                <p>Anyone can get a concussion. However, participating in <a href="https://uthscsa.staywellhealthlibrary.com/Search/160,29">contact sports</a>, such as football, or aerial sports, such as cheerleading, increases your risk. </p>
                <p>Common causes of concussions include:</p>
                <div class="indent-list">
                    <ul>
                        <li>Assaults</li>
                        <li>Collision with a person, an object or the ground</li>
                        <li>Explosive blasts from gunfire or military combat</li>
                        <li>Falls</li>
                        <li>Vehicle, scooter or bicycle accidents</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <h3>What are the symptoms of a concussion?</h3>
                <p>Concussion symptoms can appear within minutes of an injury, or hours or days later. The symptoms can also change or worsen over time. </p>
                <p>Symptoms of a concussion may include:</p>
                <div class="indent-list">
                    <ul>
                        <li>Difficulty concentrating or remembering things</li>
                        <li>Dizziness or balance problems</li>
                        <li>Headaches</li>
                        <li>Impaired vision</li>
                        <li>Nausea and vomiting </li>
                        <li>Personality or mood changes, including irritability, anxiety and depression</li>
                        <li>Sensitivity to lights and sounds</li>
                        <li>Sleeping too much or difficulty sleeping</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <h3>What are persisting symptoms after concussion (PSaC)? </h3>
                <p>For nine out of 10 people, concussion symptoms improve within 14 days. But some people develop persisting symptoms after concussion (PSaC). Previously called post-concussion syndrome, this condition means you still have at least one concussion symptom four weeks or longer after the injury.</p>
                <p>You’re more likely to have PSaC if you:</p>
                <div class="indent-list">
                    <ul>
                        <li>Continue to engage in physical activity instead of resting after getting a concussion</li>
                        <li>Don’t follow concussion recovery protocols </li>
                        <li>Get another concussion during recovery </li>
                        <li>Have a history of <a href="https://uthscsa.staywellhealthlibrary.com/Search/85,P00784">headaches</a>, <a href="https://uthscsa.staywellhealthlibrary.com/Search/85,P00814">migraines</a>, <a href="https://uthscsa.staywellhealthlibrary.com/Search/85,P00736">anxiety</a> or <a href="https://uthscsa.staywellhealthlibrary.com/Search/85,P01512">depression</a></li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <h2>Why choose UT Health San Antonio for concussion management?</h2>
                <p>As our patient, you or your child receives the same high-level concussion care that our <a href="https://uthscsa.edu/physicians/sports-medicine">sports medicine</a> doctors provide to professional and collegiate athletes in San Antonio. </p>
                <p>We offer:</p>
                <div class="indent-list">
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Fast concussion care:</strong> National organizations recommend seeing a concussion specialist within two days of an injury. We make it easier to access this care with walk-in appointments at our <a href="https://uthscsa.edu/physicians/services/saturday-sports-medicine-clinic">Saturday Sports Medicine Clinic</a> and scheduled weekday appointments.</li>
                        <li><strong>Support for athletes and students:</strong> We work closely with coaches and athletic trainers to ensure a safe return to sports. We also work with schools to ensure that children get the support they need to keep up with their schoolwork during recovery.</li>
                        <li><strong>Injury prevention program:</strong> You can’t always prevent a concussion. However, our <a href="https://uthscsa.edu/physicians/services/injury-prevention">Sports Injury Prevention Program</a> provides conditioning and training that helps lower your overall injury risk.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <h2>Diagnosing a concussion</h2>
                <p>Your treatment begins with an exam to learn more about the injury and symptoms. This information helps our team determine the most effective ways to help.</p>
                <p>Tests that help your care team determine how severe a concussion may be include:</p>
                <div class="indent-list">
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Concussion test:</strong> A series of oral and written tests help gauge the effects of a concussion on memory, problem-solving, speech and other brain functions.</li>
                        <li><strong>Neurological exam:</strong> In addition to a physical exam, your doctor will perform tests to assess your nervous system. These tests check for issues with balance, coordination, reflexes, vision and hearing.</li>
                        <li><strong>Neuropsychological assessments:</strong> If a concussion greatly affects your mood, behavior or personality, you may see one of our <a href="https://uthscsa.edu/physicians/services/neuropsychology">neuropsychologists</a> for care.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <h2>Managing a concussion</h2>
                <p>Because concussion symptoms vary from person to person, we take an individualized approach to promote a safe recovery. Our team closely monitors your care, ensuring that you return to work, school and physical activities safely. We use the latest research-based treatment protocols, which differ from older advice to rest and stay in a dark room.</p>
                <p>Your care plan may focus on:</p>
                <div class="indent-list">
                    <ul>
                        <li>Low-intensity physical activity, such as walking or riding a stationary bicycle for 5 to 10 minutes, within 24 to 48 hours after the injury</li>
                        <li>Ongoing low-intensity physical activity for 15 to 30 minutes after the first 48 hours until your doctor gives the OK to increase the exercise type, intensity or frequency</li>
                        <li>Limiting screen time to no more than 65 minutes a day for the first two days after a concussion</li>
                        <li>Returning to school or work 1 to 2 days after a concussion</li>
                        <li>Sleeping throughout the night (there’s no need to continually wake someone who has a concussion)</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <h3>Treating severe or persisting concussion symptoms</h3>
                <p>If you have severe or persisting concussion symptoms, your treatment plan may include:</p>
                <div class="indent-list">
                    <ul>
                        <li><a href="https://uthscsa.staywellhealthlibrary.com/Search/85,P01164">Occupational therapy</a> to help you manage fatigue or issues with memory, attention and organizing</li>
                        <li><a href="https://uthscsa.edu/physicians/clinics/ophthalmology">Ophthalmology</a> services to treat double or blurred vision</li>
                        <li>Physical therapy to ease headaches and neck pain</li>
                        <li><a href="https://uthscsa.edu/physicians/services/vestibular-therapy">Vestibular therapy</a> to improve balance and reduce dizziness</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <h3>Returning to sports after a concussion </h3>
                <p>Our sports medicine specialists work closely with coaches and athletic trainers to help athletes safely resume their sport. We follow the six-step return-to-play protocol established by the International Concussion in Sports Guidelines. Each step takes place over a minimum of 24 hours and includes:</p>
                <div class="indent-list">
                    <ol>
                        <li>Returning to school within 1 to 2 days after a concussion</li>
                        <li>Light-intensity physical activity, such as walking for up to 10 minutes</li>
                        <li>Moderate aerobic activity, such as moderate jogging</li>
                        <li>Higher-intensity, noncontact activities, such as sprinting or weightlifting</li>
                        <li>Returning to practice, including full-contact activities</li>
                        <li>Full return to sport</li>
                    </ol>
                </div>
                <h3>What to expect after a concussion </h3>
                <p>Most concussion symptoms go away within two weeks. But it takes longer to safely resume sports activities. If you have anxiety about returning to play after a concussion, our <a href="https://uthscsa.edu/physicians/services/sports-psychology">sports psychologists</a> can help.</p>

                <h2>Your concussion treatment care team</h2>
                <p>Depending on your symptoms and needs, you may also receive care from these UT Health San Antonio specialists:</p>
                <div class="indent-list">
                    <ul>
                        <li><a href="https://uthscsa.edu/physicians/orthopaedics">Orthopaedic specialists</a></li>
                        <li><a href="https://uthscsa.edu/physicians/clinics/rehab-medicine">Physical medicine and rehabilitation specialists</a> (physiatrists)</li>
                        <li><a href="https://uthscsa.edu/physicians/primary-care">Primary care providers</a></li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
